sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "For a tree to stand without buckling under its own weight, its diameter must increase as the 3/2 power of its height, which explains why young trees are slender and old large ones stunted, and sets a theoretical maximum tree height (about 300 ft for a Kew-flagstaff pine).";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Tree trunk diameter must grow as the 3/2 power of height";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "d \\propto h^{3/2} (no buckling under own weight); h_{max} \\approx 300\\,\\text{ft} for the Kew-flagstaff pine";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter II, “On Magnitude”; topic “Height limits of trees”. Scope: trees and other tall self-supporting plant stems"
}
